## Solution

Let $n = 2015 = 5 \cdot 13 \cdot 31$ (odd). We compute
$$P = \prod_{a=1}^{n}\prod_{b=1}^{n}\left(1+e^{2\pi i ab/n}\right), \qquad \log_2 P.$$

**Step 1: The inner product.** Fix $a$ and let $d = \gcd(a,n)$, $m = n/d$, $a = d\,a'$ with $\gcd(a',m)=1$. As $b$ ranges over $1,\dots,n=dm$, the residue $ab \bmod n = d(a'b \bmod m)$ takes each value $d\cdot j$ ($j=0,\dots,m-1$) exactly $d$ times. Hence
$$\prod_{b=1}^{n}\left(1+e^{2\pi i ab/n}\right)=\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}\left(1+e^{2\pi i j/m}\right)^{d}.$$

Let $\omega = e^{2\pi i/m}$. From $x^m-1=\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(x-\omega^j)$, setting $x=-1$:
$$(-1)^m - 1 = (-1)^m\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(1+\omega^j) \implies \prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(1+\omega^j)=1-(-1)^m.$$

Since $n$ is odd, every divisor $d$ is odd, so $m=n/d$ is **odd**, giving $\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}(1+\omega^j)=2$. Therefore
$$\prod_{b=1}^{n}\left(1+e^{2\pi i ab/n}\right)=2^{d}=2^{\gcd(a,n)}.$$

(No factor vanishes because $m$ is always odd.)

**Step 2: The full product.**
$$P=\prod_{a=1}^{n}2^{\gcd(a,n)}=2^{\,\sum_{a=1}^{n}\gcd(a,n)}, \qquad \log_2 P = \sum_{a=1}^{n}\gcd(a,n).$$

**Step 3: Evaluate $f(n)=\sum_{a=1}^{n}\gcd(a,n)$.** Using $f(n)=\sum_{d\mid n}d\,\varphi(n/d)$ (Dirichlet convolution of $\mathrm{id}$ and $\varphi$), $f$ is multiplicative. For a prime $p$ (i.e. $k=1$):
$$f(p)=\sum_{a=1}^{p}\gcd(a,p)=(p-1)\cdot 1 + 1\cdot p = 2p-1.$$

Since $n=5\cdot 13\cdot 31$ is squarefree with pairwise coprime prime factors:
$$f(n)=f(5)\,f(13)\,f(31)=(2\cdot5-1)(2\cdot13-1)(2\cdot31-1)=9\cdot 25\cdot 61.$$
$$9\cdot 25 = 225, \qquad 225\cdot 61 = 225\cdot 60 + 225 = 13500+225=13725.$$

**Result.**
$$\log_2\!\left(\prod_{a=1}^{2015}\prod_{b=1}^{2015}\left(1+e^{2\pi i ab/2015}\right)\right)=\boxed{13725}.$$

